{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07936","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07936","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07936","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kimiaki","familyName":"Shirahama","name":"Shirahama, Kimiaki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaduki","familyName":"Yamashita","name":"Yamashita, Kaduki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miki","familyName":"Yanobu","name":"Yanobu, Miki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miho","familyName":"Ohsaki","name":"Ohsaki, Miho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Feature Space Analysis by Guided Diffusion Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T17:18:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:55:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-29T01:47:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-30T01:27:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-06T05:27:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-07T00:41:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper aims to analyse the feature space of a vision-related Deep Neural Network (DNN) by proposing a decoder that can generate an image whose feature closely matches a user-specified feature. Supported by quantitative evidence of its high feature-matching accuracy, our decoder facilitates precise analysis of the DNN's feature space. Our decoder is implemented as a guided diffusion model that guides the image generation of a pre-trained diffusion model to minimise the Euclidean distance between the feature of a clean image estimated at each step and the user-specified feature. The key advantages of our decoder are its training-free applicability to analyse the feature spaces of different DNNs and its practical feasibility on a single COTS GPU. The experiments targeting CLIP's image encoder and ResNet-50 demonstrate the effectiveness of our decoder both as a feature-matching image generator and as a visual feature space analyser. The codes and data are available at https://github.com/ccilab-doshisha/FeatDec"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ACCV 2026, 27 pages, 13 figures, 1 table, codes: https://github.com/ccilab-doshisha/FeatDec"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07936","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:53:09Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:53:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-07T03:15:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07880","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07880","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07880","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"J. T. Y.","familyName":"Chu","name":"Chu, J. T. Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"J. W. D.","familyName":"Halliday","name":"Halliday, J. W. D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"C.","familyName":"Heaton","name":"Heaton, C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"K.","familyName":"Moczulski","name":"Moczulski, K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A.","familyName":"Blazevic","name":"Blazevic, A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"D.","familyName":"Schumacher","name":"Schumacher, D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M.","familyName":"Metternich","name":"Metternich, M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"H.","familyName":"Nazary","name":"Nazary, H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"C. D.","familyName":"Arrowsmith","name":"Arrowsmith, C. D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A. R.","familyName":"Bell","name":"Bell, A. R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"K. A.","familyName":"Beyer","name":"Beyer, K. A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A. F. A.","familyName":"Bott","name":"Bott, A. F. A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"T.","familyName":"Campbell","name":"Campbell, T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"E.","familyName":"Hansen","name":"Hansen, E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"D. Q.","familyName":"Lamb","name":"Lamb, D. Q.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"F.","familyName":"Miniati","name":"Miniati, F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"P.","familyName":"Neumayer","name":"Neumayer, P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"C. A. J.","familyName":"Palmer","name":"Palmer, C. A. J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"B.","familyName":"Reville","name":"Reville, B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A.","familyName":"Reyes","name":"Reyes, A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"S.","familyName":"Sarkar","name":"Sarkar, S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A.","familyName":"Scopatz","name":"Scopatz, A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"C.","familyName":"Spindloe","name":"Spindloe, C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"C. B.","familyName":"Stuart","name":"Stuart, C. B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"H.","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"P.","familyName":"Tzeferacos","name":"Tzeferacos, P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"R.","familyName":"Bingham","name":"Bingham, R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"G.","familyName":"Gregori","name":"Gregori, G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Measurement of ion acceleration and diffusion in a laser-driven magnetized plasma"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Plasma Physics (physics.plasm-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T16:00:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:52:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-28T19:42:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-02T01:18:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1038/s41467-026-70113-y","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Here we present results from an experiment performed at the GSI Helmholtz Centre for Heavy Ion Research. A mono-energetic beam of chromium ions with initial energies of $\\sim 450$ MeV was fired through a magnetized interaction region formed by the collision of two counter-propagating laser-ablated plasma jets. While laser interferometry revealed the absence of strong fluid-scale turbulence, acceleration and diffusion of the beam ions was driven by wave-particle interactions. A possible mechanism is particle acceleration by electrostatic, short scale length kinetic turbulence, such as the lower-hybrid drift instability."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07880","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-10T01:51:52Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:51:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-02T03:27:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07458","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07458","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07458","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yuhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hongyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Catharine W. K.","familyName":"Lo","name":"Lo, Catharine W. K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Parameter Identification in Reaction-Diffusion-Chemotaxis Systems from Single Turing Pattern Amplitudes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Biological Physics (physics.bio-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Biomolecules (q-bio.BM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cell Behavior (q-bio.CB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"Primary: 35R30, 35B36, 35K57, 35Q92, Secondary: 35B10, 35K10, 35K57, 92-10, 92C15, 92C37, 92C70, 92D25","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T07:26:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:26:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-10T12:57:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-11T00:50:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.3934/ipi.2026061","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Turing patterns encode key information about biological mechanisms, yet traditional inverse problems rely on non-biological data like boundary measurements, neglecting the patterns themselves. We introduce a new direction that directly uses the amplitudes of Turing patterns for parameter identification. Motivated by stripe-like biological patterns, we study two 1D models: one with density-dependent chemotaxis ($χ(n,c)=χ_0 n$) and one with ratio-dependent chemotaxis ($χ(n,c)=χ_0 n/c$). We present a framework that uses the spatial amplitude profile of a stationary pattern to recover system parameters, including wavelength, diffusion constants, and chemotactic and kinetic coefficients---offering a biologically grounded paradigm for reverse-engineering pattern formation.\n The core contribution is a proof-of-concept showing that, under a Fourier truncation at mode $M=3$, the amplitude data can reconstruct the parameter combinations $\\{d_n k^2,\\, d_c k^2,\\, χ_0 k^2,\\, r\\}$ for both models. These uniquely determine the ratios $d_c/d_n$ and $χ_0/d_n$, while individual parameters are recovered up to an overall scale. This is a purely theoretical work; numerical validation and stability analysis are left for future research. The framework is extensible to other models with suitable modifications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Keywords: Inverse reaction-diffusion equations, Turing patterns, Turing instability, periodic solutions, sinusoidal form"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07458","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-10T01:41:45Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:41:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-11T02:36:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07295","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07295","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07295","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ji","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Ji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Trevor","familyName":"Darrell","name":"Darrell, Trevor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luke","familyName":"Zettlemoyer","name":"Zettlemoyer, Luke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"XuDong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, XuDong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reconstruction Alignment Improves Unified Multimodal Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-08T23:59:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:13:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T21:03:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-19T00:08:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-27T07:29:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:27:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-25T06:17:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06-26T00:27:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Unified multimodal models (UMMs) unify visual understanding and generation within a single architecture. However, conventional training relies on image-text pairs (or sequences) whose captions are typically sparse and miss fine-grained visual details, even when they use hundreds of words to describe a simple image. We introduce Reconstruction Alignment (RECA), a resource-efficient post-training method that leverages visual understanding encoder embeddings as dense \"text prompts\", providing rich supervision without captions. Concretely, RECA conditions a UMM on its own visual understanding embeddings and optimizes it to reconstruct the input image with a self-supervised reconstruction loss, thereby realigning understanding and generation. Despite its simplicity, RECA is broadly applicable: across autoregressive, masked-autoregressive, and diffusion-based UMMs, it consistently improves generation and editing fidelity. With only 27 GPU hours, post-training with RECA substantially improves image generation performance on GenEval (0.73 $\\rightarrow$ 0.90) and DPGBench (80.93 $\\rightarrow$ 88.15), while also boosting editing benchmarks (ImgEdit 3.38 $\\rightarrow$ 3.75, GEdit 6.94 $\\rightarrow$ 7.27). Notably, RECA surpasses much larger open-source models and applies broadly across diverse UMM architectures, establishing it as an efficient and general post-training alignment strategy for UMMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"43 pages, 36 figures and 14 tables; accepted by ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07295","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:37:56Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:37:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-26T02:16:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07803","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07803","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07803","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antonio","familyName":"Agresti","name":"Agresti, Antonio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mark","familyName":"Veraar","name":"Veraar, Mark","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Note on a threshold for temporal regularity of stochastic PDEs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T14:44:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:48:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-31T16:09:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-01T01:07:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-29T16:20:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-01T01:13:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We consider solutions to linear parabolic SPDEs of the form \\[ \\mathrm{d} u(t) + A u(t)\\, \\mathrm{d} t = g(t)\\, \\mathrm{d} β, \\qquad u(0)=0, \\] where $A$ is a positive, invertible, and self-adjoint operator on a Hilbert space $X$, $β$ is a one-dimensional Brownian motion, and $g(t)\\equiv x\\in X$. We show that, for all $α\\in [0,\\frac{1}{2}),$ \\[ u\\in L^2(Ω;W^{α,2}(0,T;\\mathsf{D}(A^{1/2}))) \\quad \\text{ if and only if }\\quad x\\in \\mathsf{D}(A^α). \\] In particular, there is a lack of persistence of temporal regularity from the diffusion coefficient $g$ to the solution, and additional spatial regularity is required to improve time regularity. In particular, this provides a counterexample to a conjectured time-regularity property for monotone stochastic evolution equations posed by D. Breit and M. Hofmanová in [C. R. Math. Acad. Sci. Paris 354 (2016), 33-37]."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, to appear in Comptes Rendus - S\\'erie Math\\'ematique"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07803","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:50:03Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:50:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-01T02:32:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07405","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07405","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07405","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rihab Ben","familyName":"Belgacem","name":"Belgacem, Rihab Ben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamed","familyName":"Majdoub","name":"Majdoub, Mohamed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fujita Phenomenon for a Mixed Local-Nonlocal Hardy-Hénon Equation with Regularly Varying Time Weights"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T05:35:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:22:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-11T04:41:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:15:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-20T06:54:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-21T03:03:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-28T13:49:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-29T00:53:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-05-12T02:52:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-05-13T00:27:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We investigate the Cauchy problem for a semilinear parabolic equation driven by a mixed local-nonlocal diffusion operator of the form \\[ \\partial_t u - (Δ- (-Δ)^{\\mathsf{s}})u = \\mathsf{h}(t)|x|^{-b}|u|^p + t^\\varrho \\mathbf{w}(x), \\qquad (x,t)\\in \\mathbb{R}^N\\times (0,\\infty), \\] where $\\mathsf{s}\\in (0,1)$, $p\u0026gt;1$, $b\\geq 0$, and $\\varrho\u0026gt;-1$. The function $\\mathsf{h}(t)$ is assumed to belong to the generalized class of regularly varying functions, while $\\mathbf{w}$ is a prescribed spatial source. We first revisit the unforced case and establish sharp blow-up and global existence criteria in terms of the critical Fujita exponent, thereby extending earlier results to the wider class of time-dependent coefficients. For the forced problem, we derive nonexistence of global weak solutions under suitable growth conditions on $\\mathsf{h}$ and integrability assumptions on $\\mathbf{w}$. Furthermore, we provide sufficient smallness conditions on the initial data and the forcing term ensuring global-in-time mild solutions. Our analysis combines semigroup estimates for the mixed operator, test function methods, and asymptotic properties of regularly varying functions. To our knowledge, this is the first study addressing blow-up phenomena for nonlinear diffusion equations with such a class of time-dependent coefficients."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The proof of Theorem 1.6 has been revised, and the overall presentation has been improved"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07405","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:40:31Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:40:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-13T02:56:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07537","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07537","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07537","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michał","familyName":"Balcerek","name":"Balcerek, Michał","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adrian","familyName":"Pacheco-Pozo","name":"Pacheco-Pozo, Adrian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Agnieszka","familyName":"Wyłomańska","name":"Wyłomańska, Agnieszka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Krzysztof","familyName":"Burnecki","name":"Burnecki, Krzysztof","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Diego","familyName":"Krapf","name":"Krapf, Diego","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Two-dimensional fractional Brownian motion: Analysis in time and frequency domains"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Statistical Mechanics (cond-mat.stat-mech)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T09:14:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:32:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-29T13:27:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-12T02:31:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1088/1751-8121/ae59d1","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This article introduces a novel construction of the two-dimensional fractional Brownian motion (2D fBm) with dependent components. Unlike similar models discussed in the literature, our approach uniquely accommodates the full range of model parameters and explicitly incorporates cross-dependencies and anisotropic scaling through a matrix-valued Hurst operator. We thoroughly analyze the theoretical properties of the proposed causal and well-balanced 2D fBm versions, deriving their auto- and cross-covariance structures in both time and frequency domains. In particular, we present the power spectral density of these processes and their increments. Our analytical findings are validated with numerical simulations. This work provides a comprehensive framework for modeling anomalous diffusion phenomena in multidimensional systems where component interdependencies are crucial."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"30 pages, 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07537","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-10T01:43:36Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:43:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-12T05:02:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07966","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07966","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07966","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boammani Aser","familyName":"Lompo","name":"Lompo, Boammani Aser","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marc","familyName":"Haraoui","name":"Haraoui, Marc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Visual-TableQA: Open-Domain Benchmark for Reasoning over Table Images"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T17:52:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:56:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-21T15:48:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-22T01:07:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Visual reasoning over structured data such as tables is a critical capability for modern vision-language models (VLMs), yet current benchmarks remain limited in scale, diversity, or reasoning depth, especially when it comes to rendered table images. Addressing this gap, we introduce Visual-TableQA, a large-scale, open-domain multimodal dataset specifically designed to evaluate and enhance visual reasoning over complex tabular data. Our generation pipeline is modular, scalable, and fully autonomous, involving multiple reasoning LLMs collaborating across distinct roles: generation, validation, and inspiration. Visual-TableQA comprises 2.5k richly structured LaTeX-rendered tables and 6k reasoning-intensive QA pairs, all produced at a cost of under USD 100. To promote diversity and creativity, our pipeline performs multi-model collaborative data generation via cross-model prompting ('inspiration') and LLM-jury filtering. Stronger models seed layouts and topics that weaker models elaborate, collectively distilling diverse reasoning patterns and visual structures into the dataset. Empirical results show that models fine-tuned on Visual-TableQA generalize robustly to external benchmarks, outperforming several proprietary models despite the dataset's synthetic nature. The full pipeline and resources are publicly available at https://github.com/AI-4-Everyone/Visual-TableQA."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at the First Workshop on Foundations of Reasoning in Language Models, NeurIPS 2025. Available at: https://openreview.net/forum?id=fvJRsGwhPf"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07966","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:53:51Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:53:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-22T02:29:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07435","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07435","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07435","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ze-Xin","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Ze-Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxiong","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Jiaxiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Liu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinjie","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xinjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Sui","name":"Sui, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhizhong","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Zhizhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DreamLifting: A Plug-in Module Lifting MV Diffusion Models for 3D Asset Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T06:43:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:24:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-06T04:23:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-07T01:12:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/tvcg.2026.3679384","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The labor- and experience-intensive creation of 3D assets with physically based rendering (PBR) materials demands an autonomous 3D asset creation pipeline. However, most existing 3D generation methods focus on geometry modeling, either baking textures into simple vertex colors or leaving texture synthesis to post-processing with image diffusion models. To achieve end-to-end PBR-ready 3D asset generation, we present Lightweight Gaussian Asset Adapter (LGAA), a novel framework that unifies the modeling of geometry and PBR materials by exploiting multi-view (MV) diffusion priors from a novel perspective. The LGAA features a modular design with three components. Specifically, the LGAA Wrapper reuses and adapts network layers from MV diffusion models, which encapsulate knowledge acquired from billions of images, enabling better convergence in a data-efficient manner. To incorporate multiple diffusion priors for geometry and PBR synthesis, the LGAA Switcher aligns multiple LGAA Wrapper layers encapsulating different knowledge. Then, a tamed variational autoencoder (VAE), termed LGAA Decoder, is designed to predict 2D Gaussian Splatting (2DGS) with PBR channels. Finally, we introduce a dedicated post-processing procedure to effectively extract high-quality, relightable mesh assets from the resulting 2DGS. Extensive quantitative and qualitative experiments demonstrate the superior performance of LGAA with both text- and image-conditioned MV diffusion models. Additionally, the modular design enables flexible incorporation of multiple diffusion priors, and the knowledge-preserving scheme effectively preseves the 2D priors learned on massive image dataset, which leads to data efficient finetuning to lift the MV diffuison models for 3D generation with merely 69k multi-view instances."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages, 9 figures, TVCG 2026, project page: https://zx-yin.github.io/dreamlifting/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07435","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-10T01:41:13Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:41:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-07T03:25:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07945","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07945","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07945","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Pu","name":"Pu, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yazhe","familyName":"Niu","name":"Niu, Yazhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jia","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Jia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyu","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Junyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuai","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Shuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongsheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hongsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"One Model for All Tasks: Leveraging Efficient World Models in Multi-Task Planning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T17:27:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:55:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-25T11:04:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-26T00:40:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-06T09:10:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-09T00:33:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In heterogeneous multi-task decision-making, tasks not only exhibit diverse observation and action spaces but also vary substantially in their underlying complexities. While conventional multi-task world models like UniZero excel in single-task settings, we find that when handling a broad and diverse suite of tasks, gradient conflicts and the loss of model plasticity often constrain their sample efficiency. In this work, we address these challenges from two complementary perspectives: the single learning iteration and the overall learning process. First, to mitigate the gradient conflicts, we systematically investigate key architectural designs for extending UniZero. Our investigation identifies a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) architecture as the most effective approach. We demonstrate, both theoretically and empirically, that this architecture alleviates gradient conflicts by routing task-specific representations to specialized sub-networks. This finding leads to our proposed model, \\textit{ScaleZero}. Second, to dynamically allocate model capacity throughout the learning process, we introduce an online Dynamic Parameter Scaling (DPS) strategy. This strategy progressively integrates LoRA adapters in response to task-specific progress, enabling adaptive knowledge retention and parameter expansion. Evaluations on a diverse set of standard benchmarks (Atari, DMC, Jericho) demonstrate that ScaleZero, utilizing solely online reinforcement learning with one model, performs on par with specialized single-task agents. With the DPS strategy, it remains competitive while using just 71.5% of the environment interactions. These findings underscore the potential of ScaleZero for effective multi-task planning. Our code is available at \\textcolor{magenta}{https://github.com/opendilab/LightZero}."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"55 pages, 20 figures. Accepted as a conference paper at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07945","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:53:22Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:53:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-09T02:08:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07582","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07582","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07582","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Horacio","familyName":"Serna","name":"Serna, Horacio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"C. Miguel Barriuso","familyName":"G.","name":"G., C. Miguel Barriuso","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ignacio","familyName":"Pagonabarraga","name":"Pagonabarraga, Ignacio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Polin","name":"Polin, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chantal","familyName":"Valeriani","name":"Valeriani, Chantal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Sorting of binary active-passive mixtures in designed microchannels"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Soft Condensed Matter (cond-mat.soft)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T10:46:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:51:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1039/d5sm00806a","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Mixtures of active and passive particles are ubiquitous at the microscale. Many essential microbial processes involve interactions with dead or immotile cells or passive crowders. When passive objects are immersed in active baths, their transport properties are enhanced and can be tuned by controlling active agents' spatial and orientational distribution. Active-passive mixtures provide a platform to explore fundamental questions about the emergent behaviour of passive objects under simultaneous thermal and active noise and a foundation for technological applications in cargo delivery and bioremediation. In this work, we use computational simulations to study an active-passive mixture confined in microchannels designed with funnel-like obstacles that selectively allow the passage of passive particles. Active particles follow overdamped Langevin translational dynamics and run-and-tumble rotational dynamics. We find that adjusting the tumbling rate of active agents and the microchannel geometry leads to a maximum enhancement of the transport properties of the passive particles (diffusion coefficient and advective velocity) that correlates with the highest mixture sorting efficiency and the shortest response time."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07582","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-10T01:44:39Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:44:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-24T03:58:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07363","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07363","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07363","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qifei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Qifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuo","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Shuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhijin","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Zhijin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaodong","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xiaodong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meixia","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Meixia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Knowledge Distillation Driven Semantic NOMA for Image Transmission with Diffusion Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T03:20:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:19:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-09T09:35:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-10T01:34:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-26T02:38:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-27T02:06:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As a promising 6G enabler beyond conventional bit-level transmission, semantic communication can considerably reduce required bandwidth resources, while its combination with multiple access requires further exploration. This paper proposes a knowledge distillation-driven and diffusion-enhanced (KDD) semantic non-orthogonal multiple access (NOMA), named KDD-SemNOMA, for multi-user uplink wireless image transmission. Specifically, to ensure robust feature transmission across diverse transmission conditions, we firstly develop a ConvNeXt-based deep joint source and channel coding architecture with enhanced adaptive feature module. This module incorporates signal-to-noise ratio and channel state information to dynamically adapt to additive white Gaussian noise and Rayleigh fading channels. Furthermore, to improve image restoration quality without inference overhead, we introduce a two-stage knowledge distillation strategy, i.e., a teacher model, trained on interference-free orthogonal transmission, guides a student model via feature affinity distillation and cross-head prediction distillation. Moreover, a diffusion model-based refinement stage leverages generative priors to transform initial SemNOMA outputs into high-fidelity images with enhanced perceptual quality. Extensive experiments on CIFAR-10 and FFHQ-256 datasets demonstrate superior performance over state-of-the-art methods, delivering satisfactory reconstruction performance even at extremely poor channel conditions. These results highlight the advantages in both pixel-level accuracy and perceptual metrics, effectively mitigating interference and enabling high-quality image recovery."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages, accepted by IEEE Transactions on Wireless Communications"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07363","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:39:31Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:39:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-27T04:33:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07530","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07530","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07530","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kiet T.","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Kiet T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chanhyuk","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Chanhyuk","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donggyun","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Donggyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong Hoon","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Dong Hoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seunghoon","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Seunghoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Universal Few-Shot Spatial Control for Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T09:08:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:32:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-14T06:19:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-15T01:21:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Spatial conditioning in pretrained text-to-image diffusion models has significantly improved fine-grained control over the structure of generated images. However, existing control adapters exhibit limited adaptability and incur high training costs when encountering novel spatial control conditions that differ substantially from the training tasks. To address this limitation, we propose Universal Few-Shot Control (UFC), a versatile few-shot control adapter capable of generalizing to novel spatial conditions. Given a few image-condition pairs of an unseen task and a query condition, UFC leverages the analogy between query and support conditions to construct task-specific control features, instantiated by a matching mechanism and an update on a small set of task-specific parameters. Experiments on six novel spatial control tasks show that UFC, fine-tuned with only 30 annotated examples of novel tasks, achieves fine-grained control consistent with the spatial conditions. Notably, when fine-tuned with 0.1% of the full training data, UFC achieves competitive performance with the fully supervised baselines in various control tasks. We also show that UFC is applicable agnostically to various diffusion backbones and demonstrate its effectiveness on both UNet and DiT architectures. Code is available at https://github.com/kietngt00/UFC."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07530","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:43:26Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:43:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-15T03:08:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07834","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07834","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07834","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Genming","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Genming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harald","familyName":"Garcke","name":"Garcke, Harald","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shravan","familyName":"Veerapaneni","name":"Veerapaneni, Shravan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Convergence analysis for the Barrett--Garcke--Nurnberg method of transport type for evolving curves"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T15:11:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:50:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-11T21:25:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-13T01:56:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, we propose a Barrett-Garcke-Nurnberg (BGN) method for evolving geometries under general flows and present the corresponding convergence analysis. Specifically, we examine the scenario where a closed curve evolves according to a prescribed background velocity field. Unlike mean curvature flow and surface diffusion, where the evolution velocities inherently exhibit parabolicity, this case is dominated by transport which poses a significant difficulty in establishing convergence proofs. To address the challenges imposed by this transport-dominant nature, we derive several discrete energy estimates of the transport type on discretized polynomial surfaces within the framework of the projection error. The use of the projection error is indispensable as it provides crucial additional stability through its orthogonality structure. We prove that the proposed method converges sub-optimally in the L2 norm, and this is the first convergence proof for a fully discrete numerical method solving the evolution of curves driven by general flows."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07834","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:50:47Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:50:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-13T04:25:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.06996","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.06996","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.06996","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ting","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Ting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gelei","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Gelei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runyi","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Runyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianwei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tianwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qing","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Qing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ivor","familyName":"Tsang","name":"Tsang, Ivor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Visible Yet Unreadable: A Systematic Blind Spot of Vision Language Models Across Writing Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-04T05:35:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:00:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T13:47:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:43:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-15T07:57:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-16T00:33:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-21T04:49:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10-22T00:29:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-12-01T20:23:54Z","dateType":"Withdrawn","dateInformation":"v5; arXiv admin note: This article has been withdrawn by arXiv administrators due to violation of arXiv policy regarding generative AI authorship"},{"date":"2025-12-03T01:05:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Writing is a universal cultural technology that reuses vision for symbolic communication. Humans display striking resilience: we readily recognize words even when characters are fragmented, fused, or partially occluded. This paper investigates whether advanced vision language models (VLMs) share this resilience. We construct two psychophysics inspired benchmarks across distinct writing systems, Chinese logographs and English alphabetic words, by splicing, recombining, and overlaying glyphs to yield ''visible but unreadable'' stimuli for models while remaining legible to humans. Despite strong performance on clean text, contemporary VLMs show a severe drop under these perturbations, frequently producing unrelated or incoherent outputs. The pattern suggests a structural limitation: models heavily leverage generic visual invariances but under rely on compositional priors needed for robust literacy. We release stimuli generation code, prompts, and evaluation protocols to facilitate transparent replication and follow up work. Our findings motivate architectures and training strategies that encode symbol segmentation, composition, and binding across scripts, and they delineate concrete challenges for deploying multimodal systems in education, accessibility, cultural heritage, and security."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"arXiv admin note: This article has been withdrawn by arXiv administrators due to violation of arXiv policy regarding generative AI authorship"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.06996","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:30:52Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:30:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-03T02:50:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07463","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07463","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07463","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sven","familyName":"Kirchner","name":"Kirchner, Sven","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nils","familyName":"Purschke","name":"Purschke, Nils","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ross","familyName":"Greer","name":"Greer, Ross","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alois C.","familyName":"Knoll","name":"Knoll, Alois C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DepthVision: Enabling Robust Vision-Language Models with GAN-Based LiDAR-to-RGB Synthesis for Autonomous Driving"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T07:42:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:27:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-18T13:49:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-19T01:50:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Ensuring reliable autonomous operation when visual input is degraded remains a key challenge in intelligent vehicles and robotics. We present DepthVision, a multimodal framework that enables Vision--Language Models (VLMs) to exploit LiDAR data without any architectural changes or retraining. DepthVision synthesizes dense, RGB-like images from sparse LiDAR point clouds using a conditional GAN with an integrated refiner, and feeds these into off-the-shelf VLMs through their standard visual interface. A Luminance-Aware Modality Adaptation (LAMA) module fuses synthesized and real camera images by dynamically weighting each modality based on ambient lighting, compensating for degradation such as darkness or motion blur. This design turns LiDAR into a drop-in visual surrogate when RGB becomes unreliable, effectively extending the operational envelope of existing VLMs. We evaluate DepthVision on real and simulated datasets across multiple VLMs and safety-critical tasks, including vehicle-in-the-loop experiments. The results show substantial improvements in low-light scene understanding over RGB-only baselines while preserving full compatibility with frozen VLM architectures. These findings demonstrate that LiDAR-guided RGB synthesis is a practical pathway for integrating range sensing into modern vision-language systems for autonomous driving."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07463","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:41:52Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:41:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-19T03:16:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07717","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07717","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07717","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"D.","familyName":"Bánhidi","name":"Bánhidi, D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"B.","familyName":"Barna","name":"Barna, B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"T.","familyName":"Szalai","name":"Szalai, T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"J.","familyName":"Vinkó","name":"Vinkó, J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"I. B.","familyName":"Bíró","name":"Bíró, I. B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"K. A.","familyName":"Bostroem","name":"Bostroem, K. A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"I.","familyName":"Csányi","name":"Csányi, I.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"K. W.","familyName":"Davis","name":"Davis, K. W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"R. J.","familyName":"Foley","name":"Foley, R. J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"L.","familyName":"Galbany","name":"Galbany, L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"S. W.","familyName":"Jha","name":"Jha, S. W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"D. A.","familyName":"Howell","name":"Howell, D. A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"L. A.","familyName":"Kwok","name":"Kwok, L. A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A.","familyName":"Pál","name":"Pál, A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"C.","familyName":"Pellegrino","name":"Pellegrino, C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"C.","familyName":"Rojas-Bravo","name":"Rojas-Bravo, C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"P.","familyName":"Székely","name":"Székely, P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"K.","familyName":"Taggart","name":"Taggart, K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"G.","familyName":"Terreran","name":"Terreran, G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"S.","familyName":"Tinyanont","name":"Tinyanont, S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SN 2022xlp: The second-known well-observed, intermediate-luminosity Iax supernova"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Solar and Stellar Astrophysics (astro-ph.SR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T13:18:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-12T14:30:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1051/0004-6361/202553922","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a detailed analysis of type Iax supernova SN 2022xlp. With a V-band absolute magnitude light curve peaking at $M_{max}(V) = -16.04 \\pm 0.25$ mag, this object is regarded as the second determined well-observed Iax supernova in the intermediate luminosity range after SN 2019muj. Our research aims to explore the question of whether the physical properties vary continuously across the entire luminosity range. We also investigate the chemical abundance profiles and the characteristic physical quantities of the ejecta, followed by tests of the predictions of hydro simulations. The pseudo-bolometric light curve was calculated using optical and UV (Swift UVOT ) light curves and fits with a radiation diffusion Arnett model to constrain the average optical opacity, ejected mass, and initial nickel mass produced in the explosion. We analyzed the color evolution of SN 2022xlp and compared it with that of other Iax supernovae with different peak luminosities. We used the spectral tomography method to determine the radial profiles of physical properties and abundances of the ejecta, comparing them with a set of hydrodynamic pure deflagration models. The estimated bolometric flux peaks at $8.87\\times 10^{41}$ erg/s and indicates the production of radioactive nickel as $M(^{56}$Ni) = $0.0215 \\pm 0.009\\,M_{\\odot}$. According to the best-fit model, the explosion energy is $(2.066 \\pm 0.236) \\times 10^{49}$ erg and the ejecta mass is $0.142 \\pm 0.015\\,M_{\\odot}$. The performed spectral tomography analysis shows that the determined physical quantities agree well with the predictions of the deflagration simulations, with modifications regarding the increased Na abundance and the more massive outer layers. SN 2022xlp bridges the previously existing luminosity gap, and supports the assumption of continuous variation in the physical properties across the SN Iax subclass."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07717","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-10T01:47:55Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:47:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-13T03:08:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07956","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07956","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07956","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sotirios","familyName":"Kotitsas","name":"Kotitsas, Sotirios","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dejun","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Dejun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mario","familyName":"Maurelli","name":"Maurelli, Mario","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Edwards-Wilkinson limit for a stochastic advection-diffusion PDE"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T17:43:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:55:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-17T16:47:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-20T00:55:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We consider a diffusion in a Gaussian random environment that is white in time and study the large-scale behavior of the quenched density with respect to the Lebesgue measure. We show that under diffusive rescaling, the fluctuations of the density converge to a Gaussian limit, described by an additive stochastic heat equation. In the case where the environment is divergence-free, our result can be interpreted as computing the scaling limit of the first-order correction to the quenched Central Limit Theorem."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"28 pages, minor corrections, added references, submitted"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07956","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:53:37Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:53:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-20T02:01:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07613","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07613","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07613","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fangqi","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Fangqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Surajit","familyName":"Ray","name":"Ray, Surajit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaochen","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Xiaochen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Data-Efficient Fine-Tuning of Vision-Language Models for Diagnosis of Alzheimer's Disease"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T11:36:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:38:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T07:27:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:28:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-15T05:06:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-16T00:26:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Medical vision-language models (Med-VLMs) have shown impressive results in tasks such as report generation and visual question answering, but they still face several limitations. Most notably, they underutilize patient metadata and lack integration of clinical diagnostic knowledge. Moreover, most existing models are typically trained from scratch or fine-tuned on large-scale 2D image-text pairs, requiring extensive computational resources, and their effectiveness on 3D medical imaging is often limited due to the absence of structural information. To address these gaps, we propose a data-efficient fine-tuning pipeline to adapt 3D CT-based Med-VLMs for 3D MRI and demonstrate its application in Alzheimer's disease (AD) diagnosis. Our system introduces two key innovations. First, we convert structured metadata into synthetic reports, enriching textual input for improved image-text alignment. Second, we add an auxiliary token trained to predict the mini-mental state examination (MMSE) score, a widely used clinical measure of cognitive function that correlates with AD severity. This provides additional supervision for fine-tuning. Applying lightweight prompt tuning to both image and text modalities, our approach achieves state-of-the-art performance on ADNI with only 1,504 training MRIs, outperforming methods trained on 27,161 MRIs, and shows strong zero-shot generalization on OASIS-2 and AIBL. Code is available at https://github.com/CFQ666312/DEFT-VLM-AD."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at MICAD 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07613","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:45:23Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:45:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-16T02:24:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07105","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07105","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07105","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M. E.","familyName":"Moloko","name":"Moloko, M. E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"L.","familyName":"Marchetti","name":"Marchetti, L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"T. H.","familyName":"Jarrett","name":"Jarrett, T. H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"J. J.","familyName":"Condon","name":"Condon, J. J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"W. D.","familyName":"Cotton","name":"Cotton, W. D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A. M.","familyName":"Matthews","name":"Matthews, A. M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"T.","familyName":"Mauch","name":"Mauch, T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M.","familyName":"Vaccari","name":"Vaccari, M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Probing the Infrared/Radio correlation of the full IRAS Revised Bright Galaxy Sample with MeerKAT and the VLA"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Astrophysics of Galaxies (astro-ph.GA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-08T18:00:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-15T13:33:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1093/mnras/staf1536","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study the infrared/radio correlation of galaxies in the IRAS Revised Bright Galaxy Sample using new MeerKAT observations at $\\rmν= 1.28\\, GHz$, complemented with VLA data. We classify the objects by primary energy source (Active Galactic Nuclei vs. Star-Forming) and take into account their merger status. With this, we aim to explore the effect of galaxy-galaxy interaction on the total-infrared (TIR)/radio correlation ($q_\\mathrm{TIR}$) of star-forming galaxies by comparing the $q_\\mathrm{TIR}$ distribution between isolated and interacting/merging sources. We found the median $q_\\mathrm{TIR}$ to be $2.61 \\pm 0.01$ (scatter = 0.16) for isolated galaxies and $2.51 \\pm 0.08$ (scatter = 0.26) for interacting/merging galaxies. Our analysis reveals that interacting/merging galaxies exhibit lower $q_\\mathrm{TIR}$ and higher dispersion compared to isolated galaxies, and the difference is marginally significant. Interacting/merging galaxies have redder $W2-W3$ colours, higher star formation rates (SFR) and specific SFR compared to isolated objects. We observe a significant decrease in $q_\\mathrm{TIR}$ with increasing radio luminosity for isolated galaxies. Additionally, we find the median ratio of TIR ($8 \\,μm \u0026lt; λ\u0026lt; 1000\\, μm$) to far-infrared (FIR; $40 \\,μm \u0026lt; λ\u0026lt; 120\\, μm$) luminosities to be $\\left\\approx2.29$. By examining the relation between $L_\\mathrm{TIR}$ and the mid-infrared (MIR) star-formation rate indicator ($L_\\mathrm{12\\,μm}$) employed for our interacting/merging sample, we note a strong and consistent (similar non-linear behaviour) relationship between the TIR/radio and TIR/MIR ratios. Finally, we show that already at $z\u0026lt;0.1$, $q_\\mathrm{TIR}$ exhibits a dependence on stellar mass, with more massive galaxies displaying a lower $q_\\mathrm{TIR}$."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This manuscript is 26 pages long and contains 16 figures and 3 tables. The data are provided in machine-readable format on Zenodo at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.16889781. The paper was accepted for publication in the Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society Main Journal on 26 August 2025, ref. MN-24-1207-MJ.R3"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07105","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-10T01:33:29Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:33:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-16T02:24:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07627","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07627","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07627","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruihao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ruihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LSMTCR: A Scalable Multi-Architecture Model for Epitope-Specific T Cell Receptor de novo Design"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Biomolecules (q-bio.BM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T11:55:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:38:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T11:16:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-11T00:29:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-28T16:56:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09-30T01:15:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-08T13:31:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:47:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Designing full-length, epitope-specific TCR α\\b{eta} remains challenging due to vast sequence space, data biases and incomplete modeling of immunogenetic constraints. We present LSMTCR, a scalable multi-architecture framework that separates specificity from constraint learning to enable de novo, epitope-conditioned generation of paired, full-length TCRs. A diffusion-enhanced BERT encoder learns time-conditioned epitope representations; conditional GPT decoders, pretrained on CDR3\\b{eta} and transferred to CDR3α, generate chain-specific CDR3s under cross-modal conditioning with temperature-controlled diversity; and a gene-aware Transformer assembles complete α/\\b{eta} sequences by predicting V/J usage to ensure immunogenetic fidelity. Across GLIPH, TEP, MIRA, McPAS and our curated dataset, LSMTCR achieves higher predicted binding than baselines on most datasets, more faithfully recovers positional and length grammars, and delivers superior, temperature-tunable diversity. For α-chain generation, transfer learning improves predicted binding, length realism and diversity over representative methods. Full-length assembly from known or de novo CDR3s preserves k-mer spectra, yields low edit distances to references, and, in paired α/\\b{eta} co-modelling with epitope, attains higher pTM/ipTM than single-chain settings. LSMTCR outputs diverse, gene-contextualized, full-length TCR designs from epitope input alone, enabling high-throughput screening and iterative optimization."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 main pages, 5 figures, 2 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07627","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:45:42Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:45:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:20:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07596","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07596","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07596","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yusuke","familyName":"Hirota","name":"Hirota, Yusuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryo","familyName":"Hachiuma","name":"Hachiuma, Ryo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boyi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Boyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ximing","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Ximing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael Ross","familyName":"Boone","name":"Boone, Michael Ross","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boris","familyName":"Ivanovic","name":"Ivanovic, Boris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yejin","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Yejin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Pavone","name":"Pavone, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu-Chiang Frank","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yu-Chiang Frank","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Noa","familyName":"Garcia","name":"Garcia, Noa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuta","familyName":"Nakashima","name":"Nakashima, Yuta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao-Han Huck","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Chao-Han Huck","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bias in Gender Bias Benchmarks: How Spurious Features Distort Evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T11:14:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:37:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-06T14:43:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:37:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Gender bias in vision-language foundation models (VLMs) raises concerns about their safe deployment and is typically evaluated using benchmarks with gender annotations on real-world images. However, as these benchmarks often contain spurious correlations between gender and non-gender features, such as objects and backgrounds, we identify a critical oversight in gender bias evaluation: Do spurious features distort gender bias evaluation? To address this question, we systematically perturb non-gender features across four widely used benchmarks (COCO-gender, FACET, MIAP, and PHASE) and various VLMs to quantify their impact on bias evaluation. Our findings reveal that even minimal perturbations, such as masking just 10% of objects or weakly blurring backgrounds, can dramatically alter bias scores, shifting metrics by up to 175% in generative VLMs and 43% in CLIP variants. This suggests that current bias evaluations often reflect model responses to spurious features rather than gender bias, undermining their reliability. Since creating spurious feature-free benchmarks is fundamentally challenging, we recommend reporting bias metrics alongside feature-sensitivity measurements to enable a more reliable bias assessment."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICCV 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07596","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:44:58Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:44:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:53:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07382","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07382","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07382","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi C.","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yi C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinhang","familyName":"Tong","name":"Tong, Xinhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the exponential convergence to equilibrium for ultrafast diffusion equations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Classical Analysis and ODEs (math.CA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T04:23:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-11T00:11:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose a simple proof of the exponential convergence to equilibrium for ultrafast diffusion equations in $\\mathbb{R}^n$. Our approach, based on the direct use of Poincaré inequality, gets rid of the optimal transport arguments used in \\cite{fathi2025} which are valid for Gaussian-excluded one-dimensional weights. This simplification allows us to extend their results to Gaussian measures in higher dimensions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07382","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:39:58Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:39:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-11T01:52:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07962","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07962","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07962","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zongzheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zongzheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haobo","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Haobo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuo","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenghao","familyName":"Yue","name":"Yue, Chenghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zehao","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Zehao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huan-ang","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Huan-ang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziwei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ziwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TA-VLA: Elucidating the Design Space of Torque-aware Vision-Language-Action Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T17:50:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:55:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Many robotic manipulation tasks require sensing and responding to force signals such as torque to assess whether the task has been successfully completed and to enable closed-loop control. However, current Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models lack the ability to integrate such subtle physical feedback. In this work, we explore Torque-aware VLA models, aiming to bridge this gap by systematically studying the design space for incorporating torque signals into existing VLA architectures. We identify and evaluate several strategies, leading to three key findings. First, introducing torque adapters into the decoder consistently outperforms inserting them into the encoder.Third, inspired by joint prediction and planning paradigms in autonomous driving, we propose predicting torque as an auxiliary output, which further improves performance. This strategy encourages the model to build a physically grounded internal representation of interaction dynamics. Extensive quantitative and qualitative experiments across contact-rich manipulation benchmarks validate our findings."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to CoRL 2025, project page: \\url{https://zzongzheng0918.github.io/Torque-Aware-VLA.github.io/}"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07962","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:53:45Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:53:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:53:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07957","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07957","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07957","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shunlei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shunlei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Longsen","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Longsen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiuwen","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Jiuwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingbai","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yingbai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Graph-Fused Vision-Language-Action for Policy Reasoning in Multi-Arm Robotic Manipulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T17:44:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:55:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Acquiring dexterous robotic skills from human video demonstrations remains a significant challenge, largely due to conventional reliance on low-level trajectory replication, which often fails to generalize across varying objects, spatial layouts, and manipulator configurations. To address this limitation, we introduce Graph-Fused Vision-Language-Action (GF-VLA), a unified framework that enables dual-arm robotic systems to perform task-level reasoning and execution directly from RGB-D human demonstrations. GF-VLA employs an information-theoretic approach to extract task-relevant cues, selectively highlighting critical hand-object and object-object interactions. These cues are structured into temporally ordered scene graphs, which are subsequently integrated with a language-conditioned transformer to produce hierarchical behavior trees and interpretable Cartesian motion primitives. To enhance efficiency in bimanual execution, we propose a cross-arm allocation strategy that autonomously determines gripper assignment without requiring explicit geometric modeling. We validate GF-VLA on four dual-arm block assembly benchmarks involving symbolic structure construction and spatial generalization. Empirical results demonstrate that the proposed representation achieves over 95% graph accuracy and 93% subtask segmentation, enabling the language-action planner to generate robust, interpretable task policies. When deployed on a dual-arm robot, these policies attain 94% grasp reliability, 89% placement accuracy, and 90% overall task success across stacking, letter-formation, and geometric reconfiguration tasks, evidencing strong generalization and robustness under diverse spatial and semantic variations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This paper is submitted to IEEE IROS 2025 Workshop AIR4S"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07957","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:53:38Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:53:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:53:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07920","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07920","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07920","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinpeng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jinpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixuan","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yansong","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yansong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ScoreHOI: Physically Plausible Reconstruction of Human-Object Interaction via Score-Guided Diffusion"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T17:00:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:54:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Joint reconstruction of human-object interaction marks a significant milestone in comprehending the intricate interrelations between humans and their surrounding environment. Nevertheless, previous optimization methods often struggle to achieve physically plausible reconstruction results due to the lack of prior knowledge about human-object interactions. In this paper, we introduce ScoreHOI, an effective diffusion-based optimizer that introduces diffusion priors for the precise recovery of human-object interactions. By harnessing the controllability within score-guided sampling, the diffusion model can reconstruct a conditional distribution of human and object pose given the image observation and object feature. During inference, the ScoreHOI effectively improves the reconstruction results by guiding the denoising process with specific physical constraints. Furthermore, we propose a contact-driven iterative refinement approach to enhance the contact plausibility and improve the reconstruction accuracy. Extensive evaluations on standard benchmarks demonstrate ScoreHOI's superior performance over state-of-the-art methods, highlighting its ability to achieve a precise and robust improvement in joint human-object interaction reconstruction."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICCV 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07920","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:52:47Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:52:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:52:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07862","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07862","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07862","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arlúcio","familyName":"Viana","name":"Viana, Arlúcio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Patryk","familyName":"Wolejko","name":"Wolejko, Patryk","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rico","familyName":"Zacher","name":"Zacher, Rico","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Duality estimates for subdiffusion problems including time-fractional porous medium type equations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"35R11 (primary), 45K05, 76S05","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T15:43:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:51:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We prove duality estimates for time-fractional and more general subdiffusion problems. An important example is given by subdiffusive porous medium type equations. Our estimates can be used to prove uniqueness of weak solutions to such problems, and they allow to extend a key estimate from classical reaction-diffusion systems to the subdiffusive case. Besides concrete equations involving a Laplacian, we also consider abstract problems in a Hilbert space setting."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07862","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:51:27Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:51:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:51:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07700","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07700","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07700","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["CERN"],"givenName":"Piyush","familyName":"Raikwar","name":"Raikwar, Piyush","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["CERN"],"givenName":"Anna","familyName":"Zaborowska","name":"Zaborowska, Anna","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["CERN"],"givenName":"Peter","familyName":"McKeown","name":"McKeown, Peter","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["CERN"],"givenName":"Renato","familyName":"Cardoso","name":"Cardoso, Renato","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["CERN, Warsaw University of Technology"],"givenName":"Mikolaj","familyName":"Piorczynski","name":"Piorczynski, Mikolaj","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Thomas J. Watson Research Center"],"givenName":"Kyongmin","familyName":"Yeo","name":"Yeo, Kyongmin","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Generalisable Generative Model for Multi-Detector Calorimeter Simulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Instrumentation and Detectors (physics.ins-det)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Experiment (hep-ex)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Physics (physics.comp-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T13:04:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:42:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Collider experiments, such as those at the Large Hadron Collider, use the Geant4 toolkit to simulate particle-detector interactions with high accuracy. However, these experiments increasingly require larger amounts of simulated data, leading to huge computing cost. Generative machine learning methods could offer much faster calorimeter shower simulations by directly emulating detector responses. In this work, we present CaloDiT-2, a diffusion model which uses transformer blocks. As is the case for other models explored for this task, it can be applied to specific geometries, however its true strength lies in its generalisation capabilities. Our approach allows pre-training on multiple detectors and rapid adaptation to new ones, which we demonstrate on the LEMURS dataset. It reduces the effort required to develop accurate models for novel detectors or detectors which are under development and have geometries that are changed frequently, requiring up to 25x less data and 20x less training time. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first pre-trained model to be published that allows adaptation in the context of particle shower simulations, with the model also included in the Geant4 toolkit. We also present results on benchmarks on Dataset-2 from the community-hosted CaloChallenge, showing that our models provide one of the best tradeoffs between accuracy and speed from the published models. Our contributions include a mechanism for the creation of detector-agnostic data representations, architectural modifications suitable for the data modality, a pre-training and adaptation strategy, and publicly released datasets and pre-trained models for broad use."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07700","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:47:32Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:47:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:47:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07666","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07666","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07666","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xixi","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xixi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanchao","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Yanchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nan","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Nan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruiyang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ruiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Hong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MoLoRAG: Bootstrapping Document Understanding via Multi-modal Logic-aware Retrieval"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-06T00:59:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:40:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Document Understanding is a foundational AI capability with broad applications, and Document Question Answering (DocQA) is a key evaluation task. Traditional methods convert the document into text for processing by Large Language Models (LLMs), but this process strips away critical multi-modal information like figures. While Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) address this limitation, their constrained input size makes multi-page document comprehension infeasible. Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) methods mitigate this by selecting relevant pages, but they rely solely on semantic relevance, ignoring logical connections between pages and the query, which is essential for reasoning.\n To this end, we propose MoLoRAG, a logic-aware retrieval framework for multi-modal, multi-page document understanding. By constructing a page graph that captures contextual relationships between pages, a lightweight VLM performs graph traversal to retrieve relevant pages, including those with logical connections often overlooked. This approach combines semantic and logical relevance to deliver more accurate retrieval. After retrieval, the top-$K$ pages are fed into arbitrary LVLMs for question answering. To enhance flexibility, MoLoRAG offers two variants: a training-free solution for easy deployment and a fine-tuned version to improve logical relevance checking. Experiments on four DocQA datasets demonstrate average improvements of 9.68% in accuracy over LVLM direct inference and 7.44% in retrieval precision over baselines. Codes and datasets are released at https://github.com/WxxShirley/MoLoRAG."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP Main 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07666","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:46:42Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:46:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:46:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07647","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07647","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07647","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sung Ju","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Sung Ju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nam Ik","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Nam Ik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Semantic Watermarking Reinvented: Enhancing Robustness and Generation Quality with Fourier Integrity"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T12:15:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:39:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Semantic watermarking techniques for latent diffusion models (LDMs) are robust against regeneration attacks, but often suffer from detection performance degradation due to the loss of frequency integrity. To tackle this problem, we propose a novel embedding method called Hermitian Symmetric Fourier Watermarking (SFW), which maintains frequency integrity by enforcing Hermitian symmetry. Additionally, we introduce a center-aware embedding strategy that reduces the vulnerability of semantic watermarking due to cropping attacks by ensuring robust information retention. To validate our approach, we apply these techniques to existing semantic watermarking schemes, enhancing their frequency-domain structures for better robustness and retrieval accuracy. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our methods achieve state-of-the-art verification and identification performance, surpassing previous approaches across various attack scenarios. Ablation studies confirm the impact of SFW on detection capabilities, the effectiveness of the center-aware embedding against cropping, and how message capacity influences identification accuracy. Notably, our method achieves the highest detection accuracy while maintaining superior image fidelity, as evidenced by FID and CLIP scores. Conclusively, our proposed SFW is shown to be an effective framework for balancing robustness and image fidelity, addressing the inherent trade-offs in semantic watermarking. Code available at https://github.com/thomas11809/SFWMark"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to the IEEE/CVF International Conference on Computer Vision (ICCV) 2025. Project page: https://thomas11809.github.io/SFWMark/ Code: https://github.com/thomas11809/SFWMark"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07647","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:46:10Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:46:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:46:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07554","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07554","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07554","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanjay","familyName":"Nayak","name":"Nayak, Sanjay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikolai","familyName":"Andrianov","name":"Andrianov, Nikolai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Gruhn","name":"Gruhn, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joaquin","familyName":"Miranda","name":"Miranda, Joaquin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Atomic Layer Etching of Aluminum Nitride: Mechanistic Insights from First-Principles Studies of Chlorine Chemistry"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T09:47:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:34:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Using first-principles density functional theory calculations in combination with the climbing-image nudged elastic band method, we investigated the adsorption, desorption, and diffusion of atomic chlorine and molecular chlorine on the Al terminated 0001 surface of aluminum nitride. Our results reveal that both atomic Cl and Cl2 exhibit a chemisorption character with high binding energies. Calculations revealed that the splitting pathway of Cl2 on the Al terminated AlN 0001 surface is a barrierless and exothermic process. These findings provide new microscopic-scale insights into halogen-insulator surface interactions and opportunities for new strategies in optimizing AlN atomic layer etching process in semiconductor fabrication."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07554","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:44:00Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:44:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:44:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07521","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07521","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07521","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taihui","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Taihui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rilin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Rilin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Andong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinzheng","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Jinzheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meng","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Meng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Target matching based generative model for speech enhancement"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T08:56:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:31:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The design of mean and variance schedules for the perturbed signal is a fundamental challenge in generative models. While score-based and Schrödinger bridge-based models require careful selection of the stochastic differential equation to derive the corresponding schedules, flow-based models address this issue via vector field matching. However, this strategy often leads to hallucination artifacts and inefficient training and inference processes due to the potential inclusion of stochastic components in the vector field. Additionally, the widely adopted diffusion backbone, NCSN++, suffers from high computational complexity. To overcome these limitations, we propose a novel target-based generative framework that enhances both the flexibility of mean/variance schedule design and the efficiency of training and inference processes. Specifically, we eliminate the stochastic components in the training loss by reformulating the generative speech enhancement task as a target signal estimation problem, which therefore leads to more stable and efficient training and inference processes. In addition, we employ a logistic mean schedule and a bridge variance schedule, which yield a more favorable signal-to-noise ratio trajectory compared to several widely used schedules and thus leads to a more efficient perturbation strategy. Furthermore, we propose a new diffusion backbone for audio, which significantly improves the efficiency over NCSN++ by explicitly modeling long-term frame correlations and cross-band dependencies."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07521","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:43:14Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:43:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:43:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07516","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07516","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07516","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiqing","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Jiqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin","familyName":"Leporq","name":"Leporq, Benjamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Valérie","familyName":"Hervieu","name":"Hervieu, Valérie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sophie","familyName":"Gaillard","name":"Gaillard, Sophie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jerome","familyName":"Dumortier","name":"Dumortier, Jerome","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Olivier","familyName":"Beuf","name":"Beuf, Olivier","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hélène","familyName":"Ratiney","name":"Ratiney, Hélène","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Magnetic Resonance Imaging Virtual Liver Biopsy Using Radiomics Analysis for the Assessment of Chronic Liver Disease"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Tissues and Organs (q-bio.TO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T08:51:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:31:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Objectives: The role of advanced diffusion-weighted imaging (DWI) in chronic liver disease (CLD) has not been fully studied. Chronic liver disease (CLD) is a progressive deterioration of liver functions, caused by one or more etiology. This study was aimed to investigate whether radiomics features extracted from individual or combined magnetic resonance imaging sequences, such as T1-weighted, T2-weighted images, or quantitative maps from chemical shift encoded, diffusion-weighted imaging, can effectively classify inflammation and fibrosis in CLD. Method: Seventy-seven patients with CLD were enrolled in this study. Each participant underwent both MRI examinations and liver biopsy. The biopsy procedure was applied to quantitatively or semi-qualitatively analyze several histology features, steatosis, inflammation, and fibrosis. Radiomic features were extracted, selected, reduced, and used to train the inflammation and fibrosis classification based on random forest models. The performances of classifiers were evaluated by the receiver operating characteristic curve (ROC), accuracy, precision, sensitivity, specificity, and DeLong tests. Result: The random forest model achieved the area under the curve (AUC) of 0.85 and 0.86 for inflammation and fibrosis classification, respectively. Conclusion: This study demonstrated that the MRI-based radiomics features hold potential in the inflammation and fibrosis classification."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07516","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:43:07Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:43:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:43:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07488","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07488","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07488","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bharat","familyName":"Gandhi","name":"Gandhi, Bharat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Zhan","name":"Zhan, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohit","familyName":"Nehra","name":"Nehra, Mohit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhicheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchen","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yuchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meijia","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Meijia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naisheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Naisheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fine-Tuning Vision-Language Models for Visual Navigation Assistance"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T08:08:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:29:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We address vision-language-driven indoor navigation to assist visually impaired individuals in reaching a target location using images and natural language guidance. Traditional navigation systems are ineffective indoors due to the lack of precise location data. Our approach integrates vision and language models to generate step-by-step navigational instructions, enhancing accessibility and independence. We fine-tune the BLIP-2 model with Low Rank Adaptation (LoRA) on a manually annotated indoor navigation dataset. We propose an evaluation metric that refines the BERT F1 score by emphasizing directional and sequential variables, providing a more comprehensive measure of navigational performance. After applying LoRA, the model significantly improved in generating directional instructions, overcoming limitations in the original BLIP-2 model."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07488","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:42:27Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:42:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:42:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07484","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07484","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07484","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenshuo","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Wenshuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xicheng","familyName":"Lan","name":"Lan, Xicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luyao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Luyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuai","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Shuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LINR Bridge: Vector Graphic Animation via Neural Implicits and Video Diffusion Priors"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T08:04:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:28:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vector graphics, known for their scalability and user-friendliness, provide a unique approach to visual content compared to traditional pixel-based images. Animation of these graphics, driven by the motion of their elements, offers enhanced comprehensibility and controllability but often requires substantial manual effort. To automate this process, we propose a novel method that integrates implicit neural representations with text-to-video diffusion models for vector graphic animation. Our approach employs layered implicit neural representations to reconstruct vector graphics, preserving their inherent properties such as infinite resolution and precise color and shape constraints, which effectively bridges the large domain gap between vector graphics and diffusion models. The neural representations are then optimized using video score distillation sampling, which leverages motion priors from pretrained text-to-video diffusion models. Finally, the vector graphics are warped to match the representations resulting in smooth animation. Experimental results validate the effectiveness of our method in generating vivid and natural vector graphic animations, demonstrating significant improvement over existing techniques that suffer from limitations in flexibility and animation quality."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 pages, ICIPW 2025, Website: https://gaowenshuo.github.io/LINR-bridge/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07484","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:42:22Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:42:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:42:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07472","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07472","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07472","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenshuo","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Wenshuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xicheng","familyName":"Lan","name":"Lan, Xicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuai","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Shuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ANYPORTAL: Zero-Shot Consistent Video Background Replacement"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T07:50:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:28:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite the rapid advancements in video generation technology, creating high-quality videos that precisely align with user intentions remains a significant challenge. Existing methods often fail to achieve fine-grained control over video details, limiting their practical applicability. We introduce ANYPORTAL, a novel zero-shot framework for video background replacement that leverages pre-trained diffusion models. Our framework collaboratively integrates the temporal prior of video diffusion models with the relighting capabilities of image diffusion models in a zero-shot setting. To address the critical challenge of foreground consistency, we propose a Refinement Projection Algorithm, which enables pixel-level detail manipulation to ensure precise foreground preservation. ANYPORTAL is training-free and overcomes the challenges of achieving foreground consistency and temporally coherent relighting. Experimental results demonstrate that ANYPORTAL achieves high-quality results on consumer-grade GPUs, offering a practical and efficient solution for video content creation and editing."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, ICCV 2025, Website: https://gaowenshuo.github.io/AnyPortal/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07472","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:42:05Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:42:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:42:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07441","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07441","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07441","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sangjun","familyName":"Hwang","name":"Hwang, Sangjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chan-Byoung","familyName":"Chae","name":"Chae, Chan-Byoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Node Position Estimation in Diffusion-Based Molecular Communications Using Multi-Layer Perceptron"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"C.2.1","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"94A12","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T06:56:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:25:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3760544.3765641","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper proposes a method for accurately estimating the relative position between two nodes with unknown locations in a diffusion-based molecular communication environment. A specialized node structure is designed, combining a central absorbing receiver with multiple transmitters placed at predefined spherical coordinates. Pilot molecules are released, and their absorption time and concentration are measured. By partitioning the spherical coordinate space, these spatially distinct measurements serve as input to a multilayer perceptron (MLP)-based model. The proposed method significantly improves the precision of distance and direction estimation. Simulation results demonstrate localization accuracy, confirming the effectiveness of the neural network model in capturing the underlying physical characteristics."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"2 pages, 2 figures; accepted to ACM NanoCom '25 (short paper). This arXiv version is the author-accepted manuscript"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07441","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-10T01:41:22Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:41:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:41:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07334","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07334","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07334","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunnong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yunnong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengwei","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Chengwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liuqing","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Liuqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SpecifyUI: Supporting Iterative UI Design Intent Expression through Structured Specifications and Generative AI"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T02:19:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:16:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) promise to accelerate UI design, yet current tools struggle with two fundamentals: externalizing designers' intent and controlling iterative change. We introduce SPEC, a structured, parameterized, hierarchical intermediate representation that exposes UI elements as controllable parameters. Building on SPEC, we present SpecifyUI, an interactive system that extracts SPEC from UI references via region segmentation and vision-language models, composes UIs across multiple sources, and supports targeted edits at global, regional, and component levels. A multi-agent generator renders SPEC into high-fidelity designs, closing the loop between intent expression and controllable generation. Quantitative experiments show SPEC-based generation more faithfully captures reference intent than prompt-based baselines. In a user study with 16 professional designers, SpecifyUI significantly outperformed Stitch on intent alignment, design quality, controllability, and overall experience in human-AI co-creation. Our results position SPEC as a specification-driven paradigm that shifts LLM-assisted design from one-shot prompting to iterative, collaborative workflows."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages, 12 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07334","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:38:51Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:38:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:38:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07277","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07277","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07277","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sepehr","familyName":"Salem","name":"Salem, Sepehr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M. Moein","familyName":"Esfahani","name":"Esfahani, M. Moein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vince","familyName":"Calhoun","name":"Calhoun, Vince","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Breast Cancer Detection in Thermographic Images via Diffusion-Based Augmentation and Nonlinear Feature Fusion"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-08T23:19:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:12:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Data scarcity hinders deep learning for medical imaging. We propose a framework for breast cancer classification in thermograms that addresses this using a Diffusion Probabilistic Model (DPM) for data augmentation. Our DPM-based augmentation is shown to be superior to both traditional methods and a ProGAN baseline. The framework fuses deep features from a pre-trained ResNet-50 with handcrafted nonlinear features (e.g., Fractal Dimension) derived from U-Net segmented tumors. An XGBoost classifier trained on these fused features achieves 98.0\\% accuracy and 98.1\\% sensitivity. Ablation studies and statistical tests confirm that both the DPM augmentation and the nonlinear feature fusion are critical, statistically significant components of this success. This work validates the synergy between advanced generative models and interpretable features for creating highly accurate medical diagnostic tools."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to IEEE-EMBS International Conference on Biomedical and Health Informatics (BHI 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07277","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:37:31Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:37:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:37:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07275","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07275","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07275","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giulia","familyName":"Rotundo","name":"Rotundo, Giulia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roy","familyName":"Cerqueti","name":"Cerqueti, Roy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gurjeet","familyName":"Dhesi","name":"Dhesi, Gurjeet","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Claudiu","familyName":"Herteliu","name":"Herteliu, Claudiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Parmjit","familyName":"Kaur","name":"Kaur, Parmjit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcel","familyName":"Ausloos","name":"Ausloos, Marcel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hybrid Galam--Bass Model for Technology Innovation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Physics and Society (physics.soc-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Adaptation and Self-Organizing Systems (nlin.AO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-08T23:17:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:12:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This work proposes a hybrid model that combines the Galam model of opinion dynamics with the Bass diffusion model used in technology adoption on Barabasi-Albert complex networks. The main idea is to advance a version of the Bass model that can suitably describe an opinion formation context while introducing irreversible transitions from group B (opponents) to group A (supporters). Moreover, we extend the model to take into account the presence of a charismatic competitor, which fosters conversion back to the old technology. The approach is different from the introduction of a mean field due to the interactions driven by the network structure. Additionally, we introduce the Kolmogorov-Sinai entropy to quantify the system's unpredictability and information loss over time. The results show an increase in the regularity of the trajectories as the preferential attachment parameter increases."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"23 pages, 2 figures, 1 table, 42 references, as prepared for Sustainability"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07275","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:37:28Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:37:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:37:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07213","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07213","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07213","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raja","familyName":"Mallina","name":"Mallina, Raja","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bryar","familyName":"Shareef","name":"Shareef, Bryar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"XBusNet: Text-Guided Breast Ultrasound Segmentation via Multimodal Vision-Language Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-08T20:45:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:09:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Background: Precise breast ultrasound (BUS) segmentation supports reliable measurement, quantitative analysis, and downstream classification, yet remains difficult for small or low-contrast lesions with fuzzy margins and speckle noise. Text prompts can add clinical context, but directly applying weakly localized text-image cues (e.g., CAM/CLIP-derived signals) tends to produce coarse, blob-like responses that smear boundaries unless additional mechanisms recover fine edges. Methods: We propose XBusNet, a novel dual-prompt, dual-branch multimodal model that combines image features with clinically grounded text. A global pathway based on a CLIP Vision Transformer encodes whole-image semantics conditioned on lesion size and location, while a local U-Net pathway emphasizes precise boundaries and is modulated by prompts that describe shape, margin, and Breast Imaging Reporting and Data System (BI-RADS) terms. Prompts are assembled automatically from structured metadata, requiring no manual clicks. We evaluate on the Breast Lesions USG (BLU) dataset using five-fold cross-validation. Primary metrics are Dice and Intersection over Union (IoU); we also conduct size-stratified analyses and ablations to assess the roles of the global and local paths and the text-driven modulation. Results: XBusNet achieves state-of-the-art performance on BLU, with mean Dice of 0.8765 and IoU of 0.8149, outperforming six strong baselines. Small lesions show the largest gains, with fewer missed regions and fewer spurious activations. Ablation studies show complementary contributions of global context, local boundary modeling, and prompt-based modulation. Conclusions: A dual-prompt, dual-branch multimodal design that merges global semantics with local precision yields accurate BUS segmentation masks and improves robustness for small, low-contrast lesions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 3 figures, 4 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07213","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:36:01Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:36:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:36:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07176","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07176","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07176","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhao","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Yuhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianyao","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Jianyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minh D.","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Minh D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jelle R. H.","familyName":"Ruiters","name":"Ruiters, Jelle R. H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel M.","familyName":"Cunha","name":"Cunha, Daniel M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iris C. G. van den","familyName":"Bosch","name":"Bosch, Iris C. G. van den","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Edwin","familyName":"Dollekamp","name":"Dollekamp, Edwin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mark","familyName":"Huijben","name":"Huijben, Mark","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yulin","familyName":"Gan","name":"Gan, Yulin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nini","familyName":"Pryds","name":"Pryds, Nini","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daesung","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Daesung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christoph","familyName":"Baeumer","name":"Baeumer, Christoph","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinghua","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Qinghua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guus","familyName":"Rijnders","name":"Rijnders, Guus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaoliang","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Zhaoliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gertjan","familyName":"Koster","name":"Koster, Gertjan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Ultrathin oxide freestanding membranes with large-scale continuity and structural perfection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-08T19:48:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:07:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Freestanding oxide membranes offer integration with advanced semiconductor platforms, unlocking opportunities for flexible electronics, silicon-based spintronics, neuromorphic computing, and high-performance energy technologies. Scalable fabrication of such membranes is essential for bridging fundamental discoveries in complex oxides with practical device deployment. However, the lateral dimensions of crack- and wrinkle-free membranes remain limited to millimeter scales, forming a critical bottleneck for large-area architectures. Overcoming this challenge demands strategies that preserve crystalline quality while suppressing defect transfer during release. Here, we demonstrate an approach based on a water-soluble sacrificial layer of super-tetragonal Sr4Al2O7, enabling the fabrication of ultrathin, crack-free, and wrinkle-free free-standing oxide membranes spanning centimeter-scale areas. This method is broadly applicable to a wide range of oxides and establishes a new pathway toward large-scale silicon integration and flexible oxide technologies. Nevertheless, dissolution of the sacrificial layer introduces oxygen vacancies into the SrRuO3 membranes, with diffusion depths reaching six unit cells, leading to anomalous \"up-and-down\" transport behavior. Although post-annealing can eliminate these vacancies, the required temperatures are incompatible with CMOS processes. Therefore, ultrathin freestanding membranes fabricated by water-assisted lift-off still face critical challenges for integration into miniaturized silicon-based oxide devices."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"4 figures and SI(8 figures, 1 table), it is the first version of this manuscript"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07176","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:35:09Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:35:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:35:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07124","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07124","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07124","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rebekka","familyName":"Bieri","name":"Bieri, Rebekka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rüdiger","familyName":"Pakmor","name":"Pakmor, Rüdiger","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Freeke","familyName":"van de Voort","name":"van de Voort, Freeke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rosie Y.","familyName":"Talbot","name":"Talbot, Rosie Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maria","familyName":"Werhahn","name":"Werhahn, Maria","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christoph","familyName":"Pfrommer","name":"Pfrommer, Christoph","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Volker","familyName":"Springel","name":"Springel, Volker","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unveiling the Impact of Cosmic Rays on the Disc Sizes and Outflows from Dwarf Scales to Galaxy Groups"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Astrophysics of Galaxies (astro-ph.GA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-08T18:26:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:04:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Cosmic rays (CRs) are a non-thermal energy component in the interstellar and circumgalactic medium (CGM) that can act as an additional feedback channel beyond thermal and kinetic feedback from stars and AGN. They influence galaxy evolution by altering gas properties, regulating star formation, and shaping galactic outflows. We investigate these effects using a suite of cosmological zoom-in simulations, which incorporate CR transport and feedback on top of the Auriga galaxy formation model. Our simulations span a wide range of halo masses, from dwarf galaxies to small groups ($M_{200\\mathrm{c}}= 10^{10} - 10^{13}~\\mathrm{M}_\\odot$), allowing us to assess the mass-dependent impact of CRs in a cosmological setting. We find that CRs have the strongest impact in lower-mass galaxies ($M_{200\\mathrm{c}} \u0026lt; 10^{12}~\\mathrm{M}_\\odot$), where they suppress star formation rates by up to 50\\%, reduce gas and stellar half-light radii, and drive outflows that reach higher velocities at the virial radius compared to simulations without CRs. These CR-enhanced outflows also transport more metals and magnetic fields into the CGM, leading to higher CGM metallicities, stronger magnetisation, and lower CGM temperatures. In more massive galaxies, CRs do not significantly affect star formation or outflow properties, likely because thermal and kinetic feedback from stars and AGN dominate in this regime. However, CRs still influence galaxy morphology across all halo masses by reducing the gas half-mass radius and stellar half-light radius. Finally, variations in CR transport properties, such as different diffusion coefficients or the exclusion of Alfvén cooling, significantly affect star formation, CGM properties, and outflows in lower-mass galaxies. This sensitivity makes these galaxies key environments for testing CR transport models and refining our understanding of their role in galaxy evolution."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07124","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:33:55Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:33:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:33:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07029","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07029","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07029","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Viswa Chaitanya","familyName":"Marella","name":"Marella, Viswa Chaitanya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sai Teja","familyName":"Erukude","name":"Erukude, Sai Teja","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suhasnadh Reddy","familyName":"Veluru","name":"Veluru, Suhasnadh Reddy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Impact of Artificial Intelligence on Traditional Art Forms: A Disruption or Enhancement"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-07T16:37:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:01:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.21817/indjcse/2025/v16i1/251603012","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The introduction of Artificial Intelligence (AI) into the domains of traditional art (visual arts, performing arts, and crafts) has sparked a complicated discussion about whether this might be an agent of disruption or an enhancement of our traditional art forms. This paper looks at the duality of AI, exploring the ways that recent technologies like Generative Adversarial Networks and Diffusion Models, and text-to-image generators are changing the fields of painting, sculpture, calligraphy, dance, music, and the arts of craft. Using examples and data, we illustrate the ways that AI can democratize creative expression, improve productivity, and preserve cultural heritage, while also examining the negative aspects, including: the threats to authenticity within art, ethical concerns around data, and issues including socio-economic factors such as job losses. While we argue for the context-dependence of the impact of AI (the potential for creative homogenization and the devaluation of human agency in artmaking), we also illustrate the potential for hybrid practices featuring AI in cuisine, etc. We advocate for the development of ethical guidelines, collaborative approaches, and inclusive technology development. In sum, we are articulating a vision of AI in which it amplifies our innate creativity while resisting the displacement of the cultural, nuanced, and emotional aspects of traditional art. The future will be determined by human choices about how to govern AI so that it becomes a mechanism for artistic evolution and not a substitute for the artist's soul."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07029","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-10T01:31:42Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:31:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:31:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07020","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07020","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07020","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mu","familyName":"Nan","name":"Nan, Mu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taohui","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Taohui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruoyou","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Ruoyou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shoujun","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Shoujun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ye","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hairong","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Hairong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shanshan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shanshan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Physics-Guided Diffusion Transformer with Spherical Harmonic Posterior Sampling for High-Fidelity Angular Super-Resolution in Diffusion MRI"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-07T04:31:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:01:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion MRI (dMRI) angular super-resolution (ASR) aims to reconstruct high-angular-resolution (HAR) signals from limited low-angular-resolution (LAR) data without prolonging scan time. However, existing methods are limited in recovering fine-grained angular details or preserving high fidelity due to inadequate modeling of q-space geometry and insufficient incorporation of physical constraints. In this paper, we introduce a Physics-Guided Diffusion Transformer (PGDiT) designed to explore physical priors throughout both training and inference stages. During training, a Q-space Geometry-Aware Module (QGAM) with b-vector modulation and random angular masking facilitates direction-aware representation learning, enabling the network to generate directionally consistent reconstructions with fine angular details from sparse and noisy data. In inference, a two-stage Spherical Harmonics-Guided Posterior Sampling (SHPS) enforces alignment with the acquired data, followed by heat-diffusion-based SH regularization to ensure physically plausible reconstructions. This coarse-to-fine refinement strategy mitigates oversmoothing and artifacts commonly observed in purely data-driven or generative models. Extensive experiments on general ASR tasks and two downstream applications, Diffusion Tensor Imaging (DTI) and Neurite Orientation Dispersion and Density Imaging (NODDI), demonstrate that PGDiT outperforms existing deep learning models in detail recovery and data fidelity. Our approach presents a novel generative ASR framework that offers high-fidelity HAR dMRI reconstructions, with potential applications in neuroscience and clinical research."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07020","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:31:30Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:31:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:31:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07017","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07017","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07017","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Kiruluta","name":"Kiruluta, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Priscilla","familyName":"Burity","name":"Burity, Priscilla","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Eigenmodes to Proofs: Integrating Graph Spectral Operators with Symbolic Interpretable Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-07T01:12:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:01:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce Spectral NSR, a fully spectral neuro-symbolic reasoning framework that embeds logical rules as spectral templates and performs inference directly in the graph spectral domain. By leveraging graph signal processing (GSP) and frequency-selective filters grounded in the Laplacian eigenstructure of knowledge graphs, the architecture unifies the interpretability of symbolic reasoning with the scalability and adaptability of spectral learning. Beyond the core formulation, we incorporate a comprehensive set of extensions, including dynamic graph and basis learning, rational and diffusion filters for sharper spectral selectivity, mixture-of-spectral-experts for modular specialization, proof-guided training with spectral curricula, and uncertainty quantification for calibrated confidence. Additional enhancements such as large language model coupling, co-spectral transfer alignment, adversarial robustness, efficient GPU kernels, generalized Laplacians, and causal interventions further expand the versatility of the framework.\n Empirical evaluation on state-of-the-art reasoning benchmarks such as ProofWriter and CLUTRR demonstrates that Spectral NSR achieves superior accuracy, faster inference, improved robustness to adversarial perturbations, and higher interpretability compared to leading baselines including transformers, message-passing neural networks, and neuro-symbolic logic programming systems. Spectral attribution and proof-band agreement analyses confirm that model decisions align closely with symbolic proof structures, while transfer experiments validate effective domain adaptation through co-spectral alignment. These results establish Spectral NSR as a scalable and principled foundation for the next generation of reasoning systems, offering transparency, robustness, and generalization beyond conventional approaches."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07017","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:31:25Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:31:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:31:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.06994","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.06994","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.06994","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Srihari","familyName":"Bandraupalli","name":"Bandraupalli, Srihari","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anupam","familyName":"Purwar","name":"Purwar, Anupam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VLMs-in-the-Wild: Bridging the Gap Between Academic Benchmarks and Enterprise Reality"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-03T05:54:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:00:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Open-source Vision-Language Models show immense promise for enterprise applications, yet a critical disconnect exists between academic evaluation and enterprise deployment requirements. Current benchmarks rely heavily on multiple-choice questions and synthetic data, failing to capture the complexity of real-world business applications like social media content analysis. This paper introduces VLM-in-the-Wild (ViLD), a comprehensive framework to bridge this gap by evaluating VLMs on operational enterprise requirements. We define ten business-critical tasks: logo detection, OCR, object detection, human presence and demographic analysis, human activity and appearance analysis, scene detection, camera perspective and media quality assessment, dominant colors, comprehensive description, and NSFW detection. To this framework, we bring an innovative BlockWeaver Algorithm that solves the challenging problem of comparing unordered, variably-grouped OCR outputs from VLMs without relying on embeddings or LLMs, achieving remarkable speed and reliability. To demonstrate efficacy of ViLD, we constructed a new benchmark dataset of 7,500 diverse samples, carefully stratified from a corpus of one million real-world images and videos. ViLD provides actionable insights by combining semantic matching (both embedding-based and LLM-as-a-judge approaches), traditional metrics, and novel methods to measure the completeness and faithfulness of descriptive outputs. By benchmarking leading open-source VLMs (Qwen, MIMO, and InternVL) against a powerful proprietary baseline as per ViLD framework, we provide one of the first industry-grounded, task-driven assessment of VLMs capabilities, offering actionable insights for their deployment in enterprise environments."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.06994","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:30:49Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:30:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:30:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.06992","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.06992","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.06992","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Zhai","name":"Zhai, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siheng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Siheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingjun","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Xingjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu-Gang","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Yu-Gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FedAPT: Federated Adversarial Prompt Tuning for Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-03T03:46:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:00:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Federated Prompt Tuning (FPT) is an efficient method for cross-client collaborative fine-tuning of large Vision-Language Models (VLMs). However, models tuned using FPT are vulnerable to adversarial attacks, leading to misclassification in downstream tasks. In this work, we introduce Federated Adversarial Prompt Tuning (\\textbf{FedAPT}), a novel method designed to enhance the adversarial robustness of FPT. We identify a key issue in FedAPT under non-independent and identically distributed (non-IID) settings: a \\textit{class information gap} between clients and the global model. Clients rely solely on limited local label information to generate adversarial samples for training, while the global model must defend against adversarial attacks from global labels. To address this issue, we propose a \\textbf{class-aware prompt generator} that generates visual prompts from text prompts. This generator is guided by a \\emph{Global Label Embedding} (serving as a ``beacon\") which encodes cross-client label information to create more globally-aligned visual prompts. Additionally, we propose a \\textbf{cross-layer generator sharing} strategy to enhance prompt coupling across different layers of the model, further boosting adversarial robustness. Extensive experiments on multiple image classification datasets demonstrate the superiority of FedAPT in improving adversarial robustness, outperforming existing methods by a large margin. FedAPT also exhibits exceptional generalization in cross-domain and cross-dataset scenarios, indicating its effectiveness in real-world applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ACM MM25"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.06992","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:30:46Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:30:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:30:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":48,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=1\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-09-09T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-09-10T15%3A59%3A59Z%5D+AND+%28titles.title%3A%28%22vision+language%22+OR+%22vision-language%22+OR+%22vision+language+models%22+OR+%22vision-language-action%22+OR+%22vision-language-actions%22+OR+VLA+OR+VLAs+OR+%22world+model%22+OR+%22world+models%22+OR+%22video+generation%22+OR+diffusion+OR+diffusions+OR+%22diffusion+model%22+OR+%22diffusion+models%22%29+OR+descriptions.description%3A%28%22vision+language%22+OR+%22vision-language%22+OR+%22vision+language+models%22+OR+%22vision-language-action%22+OR+%22vision-language-actions%22+OR+VLA+OR+VLAs+OR+%22world+model%22+OR+%22world+models%22+OR+%22video+generation%22+OR+diffusion+OR+diffusions+OR+%22diffusion+model%22+OR+%22diffusion+models%22%29%29"}}